// Functions and Variables adapts owner-supplied material; Conditionals is independently authored.
part 'extended_content.dart';
part 'completed_content.dart';
class Topic {
  const Topic(this.id, this.chapter, this.title, {this.goal = '', this.lesson = '', this.example = '', this.output = ''});
  final String id, title, goal, lesson, example, output;
  final int chapter;
  bool get ready => lesson.isNotEmpty;
}
const chapters = ['Functions and Variables', 'Conditionals', 'Loops', 'Exceptions', 'Libraries', 'Unit Tests', 'File I/O', 'Regular Expressions', 'Object-Oriented Programming', 'Et Cetera', 'Final Project and Course Assessment'];
const starterTopics = <Topic>[
  Topic("python-c0-t0", 0, "Running and saving Python programs", goal:"Distinguish a saved program, editor contents and an active Python session.", lesson:"Imagine a whiteboard for quick experiments and a notebook for work you want to keep. A Python shell is useful for trying commands; a saved script lets you run a sequence again. The analogy has a limit: shell history can be recorded even though live variables do not survive a fresh session.\n\nPython source describes instructions. An interpreter executes them; it does not treat Python text as machine-code digits. A .py extension is the normal convention for Python source, not an absolute requirement when passing a readable file directly to Python.\n\nIn this academy, write Python in Code and press Run. Each Run starts fresh. Python shell accepts Python commands directly at >>>; do not type python3, exit(), or operating-system commands there. Reset shell starts a new session. Script output and shell output are separate.\n\nCode and history save to your Supabase account when the save status confirms success. Save .py downloads a separate source file; it is not the same as a cloud save. On a computer with Python installed, python3 tool.py runs the saved file. That external command reads the saved file, whereas our Run button executes the current editor contents. Compare these two workflows before answering a save question.\n\nNext: use print, strings and comments to communicate a program's result.", example:"print(\"Terminal is active!\")", output:"Terminal is active!"),
  Topic("python-c0-t1", 0, "print, strings, and comments", goal:"Use strings, print output and explanatory comments without confusing syntax with data.", lesson:"A mock tool can display a status banner. print is its loudspeaker, a string supplies the message, and a comment explains intent to a reader of the source. A printed message does not prove that a firewall or scan actually ran.\n\nprint() is a built-in function. Each ordinary call ends its output with a newline. Matching single or double quotes delimit a string; those delimiter quotes are not printed. An embedded quote can use the other outer quote type or an escape such as \\\".\n\nThe escape sequence \\n represents a newline inside a string. Source characters and displayed characters are different: predict the output lines before running the example.\n\nA # outside a quoted string begins a comment that continues to the end of the line. A # inside a string is ordinary data. Comments should explain purpose or a decision rather than repeat an obvious statement. Add a short useful header comment to your project source.\n\nA SyntaxError means Python could not parse the program. Find the reported line, then inspect quotes and punctuation; changing the expected output will not repair invalid source.\n\nNext: variables give changing values useful names.", example:"# A mock status banner\nprint(\"System Initialized\")\nprint(\"Connection secure.\")", output:"System Initialized\nConnection secure."),
  Topic("python-c0-t2", 0, "Variables and assignment", goal:"Assign meaningful names and trace how rebinding changes values.", lesson:"Think of labelled notes pointing to values in a mock configuration. In Python a variable is a name bound to an object; it is not a box that physically contains all of that object. You can bind a name without declaring its type in advance.\n\nAssignment evaluates the right-hand expression and then binds the left-hand name to the result. A single = assigns; == compares. Read statements in order. If one name receives an integer value from another, later rebinding the original name does not rerun the earlier assignment.\n\nIdentifiers cannot start with a digit, contain a hyphen, or use a reserved keyword. Lowercase words separated by underscores are the normal PEP 8 naming style for ordinary variables. A valid name and a recommended style are separate questions.\n\nStrings represent text, integers represent whole numbers, and True/False are Boolean values. Boolean spelling is case-sensitive. print can display several values separated by spaces, allowing labels without requiring new formatting syntax.\n\nReassignment changes the name's reference. An old object can remain accessible through other references; you should not claim it is always immediately destroyed. Mutable shared objects are a later topic.\n\nNext: capture text with input and store cleaned results.", example:"port = 8080\nprevious = port\nport = 8443\nprint(port)\nprint(previous)", output:"8443\n8080"),
  Topic("python-c0-t3", 0, "input and string methods", goal:"Capture text and distinguish normalization from validation.", lesson:"An operator may type a mock label with extra spaces or mixed casing. input() returns the entered text as a string when it succeeds. It is not automatically a number and it does not validate a domain, username or security command.\n\nIn Script mode, supply one response per line in Program input before Run. Tests supply their own inputs. At the top level of Python shell, input() asks for a response in the shell; inside ordinary functions it uses the supplied input box in this release.\n\nstrip() returns a new string without leading or trailing whitespace. It keeps spaces between words. lower() and upper() return text with changed casing. replace(old, new) returns text with matching substrings replaced. len(text) counts the string's characters for these basic examples.\n\nStrings are immutable: a method call does not overwrite your original variable. Assign the returned value if you want to reuse it. Method chains operate on intermediate results in sequence.\n\nNormalization is cleanup to a chosen format. Validation checks whether a value meets the application's rules. Stripping or changing case alone does not prevent injection attacks, validate an address, or make arbitrary commands safe. These exercises use mock text and do not execute it as a system command.\n\nNext: convert valid numeric text before arithmetic.", example:"raw = input(\"Server label: \")\nclean = raw.strip().upper()\nprint(\"Received target:\", clean)", output:"With input \"  academy  \": Received target: ACADEMY"),
  Topic("python-c0-t4", 0, "Numbers and conversion", goal:"Convert valid numeric text and explain integer versus floating-point results.", lesson:"The text \"80\" and integer 80 are different values. Adding an integer to that text raises TypeError; joining two strings may concatenate them instead of calculating a sum. A program error is reported in a traceback—it does not mean your device crashed.\n\nint represents exact whole numbers within available memory. float represents numbers with fractional values using floating-point approximations; many decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly. Use type(value) to inspect a type while learning.\n\nint(text) converts valid integer text, float(text) accepts valid floating-point text, and str(value) produces text. int(\"abc\") and int(\"45.8\") raise ValueError. int(45.8) is different: converting that float truncates toward zero, so int(-45.8) produces -45. It is not ordinary rounding.\n\ninput returns text. Convert it before arithmetic. These exercises promise valid inputs where specified; robust handling of invalid conversion will be taught in Exceptions. Simply calling int is not a general safety check.\n\nThe example prints separate lines so you can compare a concatenated result with a numeric sum. Keep text labels separate from calculations.\n\nNext: calculate ratios and control how results are displayed.", example:"raw = \"500\"\nnumber = int(raw)\nprint(type(raw))\nprint(type(number))\nprint(number * 2)", output:"<class 'str'>\n<class 'int'>\n1000"),
  Topic("python-c0-t5", 0, "Arithmetic and formatted output", goal:"Calculate a percentage and format a report with precise output requirements.", lesson:"A mock report should make its quantities clear. A failed request is not proof of a breach; use labels that describe the actual input data. Our exercises do not scan networks or change a firewall.\n\nPython uses +, -, *, /, //, %, and **. Ordinary numeric / produces a floating-point result; // is floor division and % is the remainder. Multiplication/division have priority over addition/subtraction; parentheses make the intended grouping clear.\n\nA failure percentage is the failed count divided by the total count, multiplied by 100. This requires a positive total. Before conditions have been taught, an exercise must promise valid nonzero totals rather than expect you to invent a guard. Later exercises will specify zero handling.\n\nAn f-string begins with f and places expressions between braces. The format :.2f displays two decimal places. Display formatting does not make the underlying float exact. Strings can describe results without mixing a text label into the arithmetic.\n\nPredict the example's result, then run it. Check both the calculation and the text. An output requirement such as Failure rate: 12.50% includes spaces, punctuation and the number of decimal places.\n\nNext: put repeated operations into functions.", example:"total_packets = 105\ndropped_packets = 5\nsuccess_rate = (total_packets - dropped_packets) / total_packets * 100\nprint(f\"Network Health: {success_rate:.2f}% operational\")", output:"Network Health: 95.24% operational"),
  Topic("python-c0-t6", 0, "Functions and parameters", goal:"Define callable functions and distinguish parameters, arguments and local names.", lesson:"If a report repeats the same formatting operation, copying its code creates several places to maintain. A function gives that operation one definition and allows different values at each call. Defining a normal function does not run its body.\n\nUse def, a function name, parentheses and a colon, followed by an indented body. Parameters are names in the definition. Arguments are values supplied in a call; they are bound to that invocation's parameters.\n\nA function can display a message using print. Meaningful names should describe what it really does: formatting a mock port report is not the same as verifying that a port is safe or open. Numeric validation using conditions belongs in the next chapter.\n\nNames assigned inside a function are normally local to that invocation. A name created only there cannot be read as a global name outside it. This is a scope rule, not a guarantee that every associated object is instantly destroyed after return; references and closures can retain objects.\n\nIn the shell, enter the definition over several lines and submit a blank line to finish the block, then call it. In Script mode, include the definition before a top-level call. Our checker may call the required function with several arguments, so avoid extra top-level output when the task requests only a function.\n\nNext: return a result rather than forcing it to be printed.", example:"def log_alert(level, message):\n    print(f\"[{level.upper()}] {message}\")\n\nlog_alert(\"warn\", \"Mock login failure\")", output:"[WARN] Mock login failure"),
  Topic("python-c0-t7", 0, "Return values and program structure", goal:"Return reusable results and organize a small mock reporting program.", lesson:"Printing communicates with a reader. Returning supplies a value to the caller so it can be stored, combined, tested or displayed later. A utility function can calculate an average without deciding how a report presents it.\n\nreturn ends the current function call and sends its value back. A function that reaches its end without an explicit return produces None. Statements after an executed return on that path do not run; conditional returns can leave other paths that do run later statements.\n\nSeparate definitions, calculation and presentation. Define a function before calling it. Store a returned value and inspect it before printing. This makes a function easy to test with several inputs.\n\nA main() function can group an interface. The standard if __name__ == \"__main__\": guard calls it when a file is run as a script, and avoids calling that interface simply because the file is imported. The next chapter explains if decisions in more depth.\n\nThe chapter project is a mock request-summary utility, not a log parser or network scanner: file reading, loops and real log parsing have not been taught yet. It should use inputs, cleanup, arithmetic, functions and returned values from this chapter. Keep the file and notes as evidence, and explain two tests in your own words.\n\nNext: Conditionals lets a program choose a path based on a rule.", example:"def calculate_score(base, multiplier):\n    return base * multiplier\n\nfinal_score = calculate_score(10, 5)\nprint(final_score)", output:"50"),
];
